1 RESI Moses Nunez appears in a 1765 newspaper ad for a “found horse saddle” in Ocheesee, FL, and is described as an Indian. This was on my English family’s land, which was purchased from the Creek Indians. (And, I have spent many hundreds of hours fishing on the Blackwater Swamp, Ocheesee, FL, which is still a rather remote location.) Why would a Portuguese Jewish man, Moses Manuel Nunez, be described as an Indian? During the Spanish Inquisition, which he fled in 1733 to Savannah Georgia, Spanish and Spanish Territories, such as Florida, were by edict of the sovereign, declared to have a hands-off policy with natives. In GA he was forced to register as a free man of color or mulatto. Moses Manuel Nunez (also known as Moses Manoel Nuñez Ribeiro) was a Portuguese Sephardic Jew who fled the Spanish Inquisition and settled in Savannah, Georgia, in 1733. Born around 1700 in Lisbon, Portugal, he was the son of Dr. Samuel Nunes, a physician who played a crucial role in aiding the early Georgia colony during a yellow fever outbreak.  ￼ ￼ In Georgia, Moses Nunez became a prominent figure, serving as an Indian interpreter and an agent for the Georgia Revolutionary forces.  His involvement in Native American affairs and his mixed heritage—having children with a woman of African descent—may have contributed to his identification as an “Indian” in certain records. During that period, racial and ethnic classifications were often fluid and imprecise, especially in frontier regions like Florida. ￼ The 1765 newspaper ad referencing a “found horse saddle” in Ocheesee, Florida, and describing Moses Nunez as an Indian, likely reflects these complexities. Given his role as an interpreter and his connections with Native American communities, it’s plausible that local settlers perceived him as such. Additionally, Spanish policies at the time promoted more inclusive attitudes toward Native peoples, which may have influenced such identifications. Regarding his family, Moses Nunez had children with both Rebecca Abraham and Rose, a woman of African descent. His will, dated October 14, 1785, mentions children from both relationships, indicating a diverse family background.
