1 MISE In honor of all veterans, let’s turn attention to Major Mordecai Myers (1776-1871), a hero of the War of 1812 who was later active in veterans' organizations. As a young man, this Jewish American founded a firm specializing in brokerage and auctions in New York City. He served in the New York State militia and was Commissioned as a Captain in the United States Thirteenth Infantry in March, 1812. During the War of 1812’s Battle of Chrysler Field, he was severely wounded. As a result, he lost an arm. It was at this time he met his future-wife Charlotte Bailey who helped nurse him back to health. Myers was a founder of the Society of the War of Eighteen Hundred and Twelve and a member of the Veteran Corps of Artillery of the State of New York. Between 1834 and 1842, the Myers family lived in the brick house at 16 Broad Street, Kinderhook (today the Columbia County Historical Society’s James Vanderpoel House of History). Myers served as mayor of Kinderhook and Vice-President of the Kinderhook Bank Board of Directors. He later moved to Schenectady where he was very active in the city’s civic life. This portrait of Myers is by New York artist John Wesley Jarvis. It is in the collection of the Toledo Museum of Art. For more information, see: “To My Son: The Life and War Remembrances of Capt. Mordecai Myers, 13th US Infantry, 1812-1815,” edited by Neil B. Yetwin. Old Fort Niagara Association, 2013. A separate post links to an interview with Neil Yetwin about Mordecai Myers’s life, including his Kinderhook connections, conducted at the Schenectady County Historical Society.
